"Brand Kit 2: Clinical Noir Luxe\n\nFor clinics positioning as elite, clinician-led, outcomes-driven (a more “medical authority” interpretation while staying premium).\n\n1) Brand strategy snapshot\n\nCore brand positioning\nHigh-performance aesthetic medicine, presented with medical authority and luxury discretion.\n\nIdeal client psychology\nEmotional: certainty, protection from risk, “best-in-class standards.”\nLogical: evidence, credentials, clear protocols, transparent treatment pathways.\n\nPerceived price tier\nPremium to luxury+\n\nWhy it outperforms visually\nMost competitors overuse beige-on-beige. This introduces controlled contrast (dark base + warm light) which reads “serious, high-end, doctor-led.”\n\n2) Visual identity system\n\nColor palette (authority + warmth)\n\nPrimary background: Obsidian Navy #0B1220\nUse: hero sections, headers, premium “night mode” panels.\n\nSecondary background: Warm Porcelain #FAF7F2\nUse: reading surfaces, long-form sections, forms.\n\nAccent 1: Champagne Gold #C7A46B (from DRD) \n\nDesign Resource Document-Hanna …\n\n\nUse: CTA, rule lines, key data points.\n\nAccent 2: Stone Gray #B9B1A8\nUse: borders, dividers, secondary UI.\n\nText colors\n\nOn dark: Porcelain #FBFAF8 (or #FAF7F2)\n\nOn light: Ink #1C1A18\n\nButton/CTA color logic\n\nPrimary CTA on dark: Gold #C7A46B with Obsidian Navy #0B1220 text (high-end contrast).\n\nPrimary CTA on light: Obsidian Navy #0B1220 with Porcelain #FAF7F2 text (feels “clinical serious”).\nRule: never use bright greens/reds for CTAs; use navy/gold only.\n\nTypography system\n\nPrimary headline font: Canela (same “brand moment” restraint as DRD) \n\nDesign Resource Document-Hanna …\n\n\nRationale: editorial luxury balances the medical authority of the dark palette.\n\nSecondary/body font: Söhne (trust + legibility across dense info) \n\nDesign Resource Document-Hanna …\n\nUsage rules\n\nDark theme makes bold feel aggressive; use Regular/Medium more than Semibold.\n\nIncrease letterspacing slightly for nav labels only (not body).\n\nSpacing & layout rules\n\nWhitespace philosophy: “museum exhibit”: fewer elements, more impact.\n\nGrid style: tight grid with strong vertical rhythm (authority), but generous padding inside cards (luxury).\n\nSection density: keep sections short; use modular “protocol cards” for depth.\n\n3) Design style & aesthetic direction\n\nOverall vibe: clinical-modern luxury, clinician-led credibility.\n\nImage style: real photography with controlled lighting; darker backgrounds; macro detail shots (glass, metal, clean interiors).\n\nTexture usage: subtle gradient falloff on dark (not colorful); minimal grain.\n\nIcon style: micro-line icons, slightly more geometric (medical precision).\n\nDo’s\n\nUse charts, protocol diagrams, credential blocks.\n\nUse small “clinical note” captions under claims.\n\nDon’ts\n\nNo lifestyle influencer imagery.\n\nNo glossy “beauty salon” motifs.\n\n4) Ad & social creative direction\n\nLayout style\nDark hero card with one claim + one proof metric + one credential marker (e.g., “clinician-led protocol”).\n\nText-to-image ratio\n25–35% text is acceptable because the dark base requires clarity—still minimal.\n\nHeadline tone\nConfident clinical: “Precision-led rejuvenation. Measured outcomes.”\n\nTrust communication\nUse controlled contrast, credential hierarchy, clinical notes, and “protocol previews.”\n\nExample ad concepts\n\n“Protocol Preview” single image: dark card + 3-step protocol list.\n\n“What’s Included” carousel: each slide = one module (consult, treatment planning, follow-up).\n\n“Standards” post: sterile, minimal icons + short standards list.\n\n5) Website & funnel application\n\nHomepage hero\nDark hero with one strong line + secondary line explaining safety/process; gold CTA; secondary “See standards.”\n\nCTA behavior\nUse navy CTA on light surfaces and gold CTA on dark surfaces; never mix additional CTA colors.\n\nBefore/After handling\nPlace after standards/protocol sections. Present as a gallery with clinical context and consistent framing.\n\nForm styling\nLight form on dark page: Warm Porcelain card, crisp inputs, minimal fields, strong privacy microcopy.\n\nFear reduction\nThe design “shows the clinic’s rules”: standards, follow-up, candidacy, and what to expect.\n\n6) Why this works for MedSpas\n\nBuyer behavior alignment\nHigh-ticket buyers want authority and risk reduction. Dark luxury communicates seriousness and expertise.\n\nAvoids mistakes\nNo discount vibe, no chaotic collage ads, no overdone beige monotony.\n\nExpensive without flashy\nPremium contrast + restraint feels like a private specialist practice, not a marketing brand."